{
  "dom_id": "step-q324172",
  "name": "Question 10",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14226602/assets/ce263ef057316e67e65b3adf1a5d66797c6a97c9bdcd33cae866d9111e523e10.png)\n\nThe curves $r=\\sqrt{3}-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ and $r=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ intersect for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ at points $A$ and $B,$ as shown above. What are the polar coordinates $({r}_{A},{\\theta }_{A})$ and $({r}_{B},{\\theta }_{B})$ of the points $A$ and $B,$ respectively? Express the polar angles ${\\theta }_{A}$ and ${\\theta }_{B}$ in radians.\n\n$({r}_{A},{\\theta }_{A})=($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$),\\,$\n\n$({r}_{B},{\\theta }_{B})=($\n{{field-3}}$,$\n{{field-4}}$)$",
  "worked_solution": "To determine the polar coordinates of the intersection points, we will solve the following system of equations for $r$ and $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}r=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ r=\\sqrt{3}-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe begin by setting the equations equal to each other and solve for $\\theta .$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\sqrt{3}-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation in the interval $[0,2\\pi ),$ we obtain ${\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$ and ${\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}.$\n\nNow, we use the equation $r=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ to find the $r$-coordinates of the intersection points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{1} & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3})=-4\\,(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})=2\\sqrt{3} \\\\ {r}_{2} & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3})=-4\\,(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})=2\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar coordinates $({r}_{A},{\\theta }_{A})$ and $({r}_{B},{\\theta }_{B})$ of the points $A$ and $B,$ respectively, are\n\n$$\n({r}_{A},{\\theta }_{A})=(2\\sqrt{3},\\frac{4\\pi }{3}),\\,({r}_{B},{\\theta }_{B})=(2\\sqrt{3},\\frac{5\\pi }{3}).\n$$",
